# Superpowers Workflow Rules for AI Assistant

# When the user asks you to develop a new feature or fix a bug:
# 1. ALWAYS follow the 7-step Superpowers workflow
# 2. Use task splitting (architecture-task-splitter) to break down complex tasks
# 3. Apply TDD methodology - write tests first, then implement
# 4. Run full test suite before completing any task
# 5. Perform code review on all changes
# 6. Update documentation as needed

# === Step 1: Brainstorming ===
- Understand requirements thoroughly
- Analyze technical feasibility
- Identify risks and dependencies
- Define clear scope and boundaries

# === Step 2: Environment Isolation ===
- Create feature branch: feature/{name} or fix/{name}
- Ensure dependencies are properly installed
- Use isolated test data

# === Step 3: Task Splitting ===
- Break tasks into 2-4 hour chunks
- Assign priorities (P0/P1/P2)
- Identify task dependencies

# === Step 4: Subagent Development ===
- Use appropriate subagents for specialized tasks:
  - requirements-analyst: requirements analysis
  - architecture-task-splitter: technical design
  - senior-frontend-engineer: frontend development
  - senior-backend-engineer: backend development
  - ui-designer: UI design
  - code-reviewer: code review
  - test-engineer: test cases
  - compliance-engineer: compliance
  - document-manager: documentation

# === Step 5: TDD ===
- Write unit tests BEFORE implementation
- Aim for >=80% test coverage
- Cover edge cases and boundary conditions
- Run tests after every change

# === Step 6: Code Review ===
- Run lint and typecheck
- Ensure all tests pass
- Review code quality, performance, and security
- Fix all review comments before merging

# === Step 7: Branch Cleanup ===
- Merge to main branch
- Resolve conflicts
- Clean up temporary branches
- Update documentation
- Verify deployment

# === General Rules ===
- ALWAYS write tests for new code
- ALWAYS run the full test suite before finishing
- ALWAYS update documentation for changes
- NEVER skip any step in the workflow
- PRIORITIZE code quality over speed
- USE TypeScript strict mode for type safety
- FOLLOW the project's coding conventions
- DOCUMENT all internal code with JSDoc comments

# === Test Requirements ===
- Unit tests required for all services and utilities
- Integration tests for critical workflows
- Test edge cases and error scenarios
- All tests must pass before completing task

# === Documentation Requirements ===
- Update DESIGN_SPEC.md for new features
- Update TECHNICAL.md for technical changes
- Update CHANGE_LOG.md for all changes
- Add comments to complex logic

# === Quality Gates ===
- lint: npm run lint
- typecheck: included in npm run build
- tests: npm test
- All must pass before completing any task